  <h2>What water damage restoration in Baytown usually involves</h2>
  <p>Restoration companies do not all work the same way, but a competent job follows a predictable order. Knowing it helps you tell whether the crew in your living room is doing things properly.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Inspection and moisture mapping.</strong> A technician finds where the water came from, checks that it has stopped, and uses a moisture meter (and sometimes a thermal camera) to trace how far the water traveled. Water moves sideways under flooring and up inside walls, so the wet area is nearly always larger than the puddle.</li>
    <li><strong>Water extraction.</strong> Standing water and water held in carpet are pulled out with pumps and truck-mounted or portable extractors. This step removes far more water, far faster, than any amount of fan drying.</li>
    <li><strong>Removing what cannot be dried.</strong> Soaked carpet pad, swollen particle board, wet insulation and drywall that has absorbed contaminated water generally come out. Baseboards are often pulled so air can reach the bottom of the wall.</li>
    <li><strong>Structural drying.</strong> Air movers push dry air across wet surfaces while dehumidifiers pull the moisture out of the air. Equipment runs around the clock, and readings should be logged at each visit.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning and sanitizing.</strong> Surfaces touched by the water are cleaned. If the water was not clean, this step is much more involved.</li>
    <li><strong>Repairs.</strong> New drywall, paint, flooring and trim. Some restoration companies handle rebuilding themselves and some hand it to another contractor. Ask which applies before you sign.</li>
  </ol>

  <Figure name="waterFloor" />

  <h2>How long drying takes in Gulf Coast humidity</h2>
  <p>
    Baytown sits on the upper Texas coast beside Galveston Bay and the Houston Ship Channel. For much of the year the outdoor air is warm and already loaded with moisture. That affects drying in two ways.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, the old advice to open every window does not hold here on most days. Humid outside air cannot absorb much more water, so letting it in can leave materials wetter for longer. Restoration crews in this region normally close the house, run the air conditioning if it is safe to do so, and rely on dehumidifiers to create dry air indoors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, anything that stays damp is at higher risk. The <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA advises</a> drying water-damaged areas and items within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth. Warm, humid conditions give mold what it needs, which is why a slow start costs more here than it might in a dry climate.
  </p>
  <p>
    We will not quote a fixed number of days, because nobody can honestly promise one before measuring. As a general guide, drying is measured in days: a small clean-water leak caught early is at the short end, and a house that sat wet through a weekend, or one with wet wall cavities and hardwood, is at the long end. The right finish line is a moisture reading that matches the dry parts of the house, not a date on a calendar.
  </p>

  <h2>What kind of water is it?</h2>
  <p>The restoration industry sorts water into three categories, described in the ANSI/IICRC S500 standard for water damage restoration. The category decides how much can be saved.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Typical source</th><th scope="col">What it means for you</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1: clean water</td><td>Broken supply line, leaking water heater tank, overflowing sink with no contaminants</td><td>Lowest risk. Materials can often be dried in place if work starts quickly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2: gray water</td><td>Washing machine or dishwasher discharge, aquarium, toilet overflow with urine only</td><td>Can cause illness. Carpet pad is usually removed and surfaces need cleaning.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3: black water</td><td>Sewage backup, rising floodwater, storm surge</td><td>Unsafe to touch. Porous materials that got wet are normally removed. Leave this to professionals.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Clean water does not stay clean. Left sitting in a warm house, Category 1 water degrades, which is one more reason not to wait.</p>

  <div class="notice">
    <h3>Safety before cleanup</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>If water is near outlets, power strips or the breaker panel, stay out of the wet area and have the power shut off before anyone walks in. The National Weather Service warns that the electrical system should be off, and wiring checked by the power company or a qualified electrician, before a flood-damaged building is used again.</li>
      <li>A ceiling that is bulging with water can come down. Keep people out from under it.</li>
      <li>The EPA says damage caused by sewage or other contaminated water should be handled by a professional with experience in that kind of cleanup. Keep children and pets away.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Common causes of water damage in Baytown homes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Supply lines and fittings.</strong> Braided hoses on toilets, sinks, ice makers and washing machines fail without warning. See our page on <a href="/burst-pipe-water-damage-baytown/">burst pipes and appliance leaks</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Water heaters.</strong> Tanks in garages, attics and hall closets rust through at the bottom. An attic unit can soak a ceiling before anyone notices.</li>
    <li><strong>Air conditioning condensate.</strong> Air conditioners here run most of the year and remove a lot of water from the air. A clogged drain line or a rusted pan under an attic air handler is a frequent source of ceiling stains.</li>
    <li><strong>Slab leaks.</strong> Many Baytown houses sit on concrete slab foundations with plumbing run beneath or through the slab. A leak there may show up as a warm spot on the floor, damp flooring or an unexplained water bill.</li>
    <li><strong>Roof and wind-driven rain.</strong> Thunderstorms and tropical systems push water past flashing, vents and damaged shingles.</li>
    <li><strong>Rising water.</strong> Street flooding and storm surge are a different situation with different insurance rules. That is covered on our <a href="/flood-storm-water-cleanup-baytown/">flood and storm cleanup page</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions to ask the company you are connected with</h2>
  <p>You are hiring them, so interview them. A good company answers these without getting defensive.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Can you send proof of general liability insurance, and workers' compensation or an equivalent, before work starts?</li>
    <li>Do your technicians hold water damage training certificates, such as IICRC Water Damage Restoration Technician? Can I see them?</li>
    <li>Will I get a written scope of work and price before you begin, and written change orders if it grows?</li>
    <li>How often will you take moisture readings, and can I see the log?</li>
    <li>If mold is found, who handles it? In Texas, mold remediation above a small size threshold requires a state license. Our <a href="/mold-after-water-damage-baytown/">mold page</a> explains the rules.</li>
    <li>Do you do the rebuild, or only the drying?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Our guide to <a href="/choosing-a-restoration-company-baytown/">choosing a restoration company</a> goes through each of these in more detail.</p>

  <h2>Insurance, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    According to the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/when-are-water-damage-and-mold-covered-by-insurance.html" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>, most homeowner and renter policies cover sudden and accidental water damage such as a burst pipe or broken washer hose, and usually do not cover gradual leaks or flooding. Every policy is different, and only your insurer can tell you what yours covers. Take photos and video before anything is moved, keep damaged items until the adjuster has seen them, and save receipts. We cover this step by step in our <a href="/water-damage-insurance-claim-tips/">insurance claim tips</a>.
  </p>
